00:01:49.148 --> 00:01:50.690
DALE MARKOWTIZ: But
first, let's talk

00:01:50.690 --> 00:01:53.468
about what machine
learning actually is.

00:01:53.468 --> 00:01:55.010
The way that I like
to think about it

00:01:55.010 --> 00:01:57.650
is just finding
patterns in data, which

00:01:57.650 --> 00:01:58.868
might sound pretty simple.

00:01:58.868 --> 00:02:01.160
But you can actually build
lots of awesome applications

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:02.870
with that concept.

00:02:02.870 --> 00:02:06.290
Like for example, the algorithm
that recommends you music

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:08.600
based on your past
listening history,

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:11.900
or maybe you want to forecast
trends or build an AlphaGo,

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:14.240
a board game playing algorithm.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:17.810
Or maybe you want to do a task
called classification, which

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:20.450
I'll focus on because it's
sort of the canonical machine

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:22.730
learning application.

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:26.400
Classification is essentially
just labeling things.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:28.310
Maybe you have pictures
of cats and dogs

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:31.800
and you want to
assign them labels.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:34.650
And the way that you normally
do this is roughly the same.

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:36.160
You always build a model.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:39.957
And that model usually starts
with a label training data set.

00:02:39.957 --> 00:02:41.790
So you'll have lots of
pictures of all sorts

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:43.498
of different breeds
of cats and all sorts

00:02:43.498 --> 00:02:44.900
of different breeds of dogs.

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:46.650
And you want to have
as diverse a data set

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:48.540
as possible because the
more your model knows

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:52.290
about the world the more
accurate it will be.

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:55.110
You feed this into some
machine learning algorithm

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:57.145
and out pops a model.

00:02:57.145 --> 00:02:59.520
And, if you've done a good
job and if have a lot of data,

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:01.895
hopefully when you show that
model a new picture of a dog

00:03:01.895 --> 00:03:05.620
it's able to correctly
label it as such.

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:07.390
But if machine
learning truly is as I

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:11.050
say finding patterns in
data, that means you probably

00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:13.940
kind of need data.

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:16.550
TORRY YANG: Well, what
