00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:08.375
Let's head back to the slides.

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:16.260
TORRY YANG: All right, so we've
seen a bunch of off-the-shelf

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:17.100
API's.

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:19.210
But what about custom tasks?

00:08:19.210 --> 00:08:21.870
Let me explain to you what
I mean by Custom Task.

00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:26.970
So we've seen, given
a picture of a cat,

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:29.100
you give it to the
Cloud Vision API,

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:31.320
and you get back this response.

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:32.500
That's really great.

00:08:32.500 --> 00:08:35.970
But what if, given the
same image of a cat,

00:08:35.970 --> 00:08:39.960
you want to give a
mystery API and you

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:42.799
want to get back this
result, Maine coon.

00:08:42.799 --> 00:08:45.310
Well, this is specific
to your use case.

00:08:45.310 --> 00:08:48.940
This is not something the vision
API anticipated you might need.

00:08:48.940 --> 00:08:50.970
So, in this case,
you're going to need

00:08:50.970 --> 00:08:54.160
to train your own
machine learning model.

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:58.130
Well, it turns out this
is a fairly long process

00:08:58.130 --> 00:09:00.990
at approximately these
number of steps, right?

00:09:00.990 --> 00:09:04.380
I'll quickly walk through
some of these steps.

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:08.170
But don't worry if it's
complicated because it is.

00:09:08.170 --> 00:09:10.770
All right, so first you have
to gather and preprocess

00:09:10.770 --> 00:09:15.520
all these data and group it
into these different cat breeds.

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:21.320
And then you have to split the
data set up into a training set

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:23.690
and evaluation set.

00:09:23.690 --> 00:09:28.130
And then you have to build
your TensorFlow or Keras model.

00:09:28.130 --> 00:09:29.750
You have to build the model.

00:09:29.750 --> 00:09:32.150
And then you have
to train the model,

00:09:32.150 --> 00:09:34.770
and then you have to
evaluate the model.

00:09:34.770 --> 00:09:36.440
And, finally, with
the model, you

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:39.020
have to deploy it somewhere,
maybe in the cloud,

00:09:39.020 --> 00:09:43.280
maybe on your phone,
just so you can

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:46.160
get to the final step
of making a prediction,

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:48.863
just so you can upload
a photo into the API

00:09:48.863 --> 00:09:50.030
and get the result you want.

00:09:50.030 --> 00:09:51.800
Let's just say
Maine coon, right?

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:54.230
So in reality, you just
want to get from step one

00:09:54.230 --> 00:09:56.540
to step seven as
fast as possible

00:09:56.540 --> 00:10:00.170
without all of the
intermediate steps.

00:10:00.170 --> 00:10:03.680
Well, have you all seen this ad?

00:10:03.680 --> 00:10:04.740
It's a chore, right?

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:06.620
So you want to
make Google do it.

00:10:06.620 --> 00:10:09.650
Make Google built
your model for you.

00:10:09.650 --> 00:10:12.830
That's where Cloud
AutoML comes in.

00:10:12.830 --> 00:10:14.240
What is called AutoML?

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:17.630
Cloud AutoML allows
you to bring your data

00:10:17.630 --> 00:10:19.730
and give it to AutoML.

00:10:19.730 --> 00:10:22.970
AutoML will take care
of training, deploying,

00:10:22.970 --> 00:10:24.230
and serving your model.

00:10:24.230 --> 00:10:28.880
And all you have to worry
about is querying the REST API.

00:10:28.880 --> 00:10:32.000
And, if we go back to the seven
step process I showed you,

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:33.950
it really simplifies
the whole process.

00:10:33.950 --> 00:10:35.180
You get your problem.

00:10:35.180 --> 00:10:37.310
You get your data, and
you make a prediction.

00:10:37.310 --> 00:10:39.300
All those intermediate
steps, Cloud AutoML

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:41.625
will solve it for you.

00:10:41.625 --> 00:10:43.000
DALE MARKOWTIZ:
Today we're going

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:45.420
to build our own custom
AutoML model to do

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:48.143
a task called object detection.

00:10:48.143 --> 00:10:50.560
That's when not only do we
want to know what's in a photo,

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:52.650
but we actually want to
know exactly where it is.

00:10:52.650 --> 00:10:55.005
We want to find
the bounding box.

00:10:55.005 --> 00:10:57.630
And today we're going to build
a model that identifies clothing

00:10:57.630 --> 00:10:59.880
just like we've done on Sundar.

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:02.860
So how do we do it?

00:11:02.860 --> 00:11:06.030
First, we need to find a label
data set like Torry mentioned.

00:11:06.030 --> 00:11:08.010
And, to do that, we
went to Kaggle, which

00:11:08.010 --> 00:11:10.110
is a website for data
science competitions

00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:12.890
that also happens to host lots
of awesome free public data

00:11:12.890 --> 00:11:13.770
sets.

00:11:13.770 --> 00:11:16.840
And so we found almost exactly
the data set that we needed,

00:11:16.840 --> 00:11:18.660
a clothing item detection.

00:11:18.660 --> 00:11:20.500
So it had all
pictures like this.

00:11:20.500 --> 00:11:24.490
And the human beings had
labeled pants, shirt, et cetera.

00:11:24.490 --> 00:11:26.800
So this really helped
us get started.

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:30.120
But there was a problem because
these photos are a little bit

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:31.300
unnatural.

00:11:31.300 --> 00:11:34.180
Everybody's just standing
on this white background.

00:11:34.180 --> 00:11:36.840
And so, when we try
it on us in real space

00:11:36.840 --> 00:11:38.590
the model doesn't work as well.

00:11:38.590 --> 00:11:40.940
So, to fix this problem,
we augmented our data

00:11:40.940 --> 00:11:43.560
set by actually
uploading our own photos

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:46.710
and outlining boxes
of our clothing.

00:11:46.710 --> 00:11:48.120
And we did that for a while.

00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:50.160
But then, to make the
process even faster,

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:51.930
we sent our photos
to Google's data

00:11:51.930 --> 00:11:55.170
labeling service, which was a
feature that can have Google

00:11:55.170 --> 00:11:56.940
annotate your data for you.

00:11:56.940 --> 00:11:59.150
So, to see what
this looks like, you

00:11:59.150 --> 00:12:01.710
write a set of
instructions in a PDF

00:12:01.710 --> 00:12:03.750
of how you want your
data to be labeled.

00:12:03.750 --> 00:12:05.520
So you'll say, for
example, I'm going

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:08.550
to give you all these photos,
put a box around my jacket.

00:12:08.550 --> 00:12:10.680
But, if I'm wearing a shirt
underneath the jacket,

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:12.370
don't include that.

00:12:12.370 --> 00:12:15.260
So you send your
instructions to raters.

00:12:15.260 --> 00:12:17.760
And the results that you get
back looks something like this.

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:20.490
I'm sorry, I just dumped
this huge spreadsheet on you.

00:12:20.490 --> 00:12:23.220
But basically, it's what
we found in the photo

00:12:23.220 --> 00:12:25.680
and where the box that
surrounds it is located.

00:12:28.607 --> 00:12:30.190
Now let me show you
how we actually go

00:12:30.190 --> 00:12:32.030
from that to building a model.

00:12:32.030 --> 00:12:33.185
Let's hop to the laptop.

00:12:39.230 --> 00:12:41.230
So here I am in the
AutoML Vision UI.

00:12:41.230 --> 00:12:43.690
Now there are two different
types of models we could train.

00:12:43.690 --> 00:12:46.720
One says just labels
a photo cat or dog.

00:12:46.720 --> 00:12:49.600
But object detection like I
was telling you about actually

00:12:49.600 --> 00:12:52.490
puts the box around the items.

00:12:52.490 --> 00:12:54.610
So our first step is to
upload our data set, which

00:12:54.610 --> 00:12:57.760
I kind of showed you.

00:12:57.760 --> 00:13:03.140
Just to give you
a closer peek, I'm

00:13:03.140 --> 00:13:06.120
going to expedite
that a little bit.

00:13:06.120 --> 00:13:08.480
So here are some of our data.

00:13:08.480 --> 00:13:11.760
I'm going to hop into a
photo of our colleague page.

00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:14.180
And if we wanted to label
this data set ourself,

00:13:14.180 --> 00:13:17.090
we actually just
click and drag around

00:13:17.090 --> 00:13:19.270
the thing we want to label.

00:13:19.270 --> 00:13:21.260
And then we say this is a top.

00:13:24.740 --> 00:13:26.910
And then we save it.

00:13:26.910 --> 00:13:27.930
Great.

00:13:27.930 --> 00:13:30.210
Now, definitely
preparing your data set

00:13:30.210 --> 00:13:32.880
is the hardest part of
building a model with AutoML.

00:13:32.880 --> 00:13:35.110
Training is actually
pretty simple.

00:13:35.110 --> 00:13:37.920
We just hop into the Train tab.

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:39.930
We click train new
model, and then we

00:13:39.930 --> 00:13:41.480
can select different
features like do

00:13:41.480 --> 00:13:44.760
we want a more accurate
model or a faster model.

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:46.295
And then we click
Start Training,

00:13:46.295 --> 00:13:47.670
and then we'll
get a message that

00:13:47.670 --> 00:13:51.000
says something to the tune of
we're training a model for you.

00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:52.875
Check back in four hours.

00:13:52.875 --> 00:13:54.750
And then in four hours,
you get an email that

00:13:54.750 --> 00:13:56.370
says take a look at your model.

00:13:59.410 --> 00:14:03.040
In this evaluation tab, we can
see how well the model did.

00:14:03.040 --> 00:14:05.260
So most of the time,
it will be better

00:14:05.260 --> 00:14:07.940
at detecting some clothing
items more than others.

00:14:07.940 --> 00:14:10.965
So let's just investigate
how it did on t-shirts.

00:14:10.965 --> 00:14:12.340
There are lots of
statistics that

00:14:12.340 --> 00:14:14.230
are useful to evaluate
your model here.

00:14:14.230 --> 00:14:17.260
But I'll just show you
the most useful feature

00:14:17.260 --> 00:14:19.060
for debugging I
think, which is seeing

00:14:19.060 --> 00:14:22.800
the examples your model labeled
correctly and incorrectly.

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:26.080
So these true positives are
correctly labeled as t-shirts.

00:14:28.750 --> 00:14:30.680
But here are some
false positives.

00:14:30.680 --> 00:14:33.160
So I thought these were
t-shirts when they're not.

00:14:33.160 --> 00:14:35.230
To be honest, I think
it's kind of hard to tell

00:14:35.230 --> 00:14:36.260
the difference between--

00:14:36.260 --> 00:14:36.920
whatever.

00:14:36.920 --> 00:14:39.010
But this will help
you debug your model

00:14:39.010 --> 00:14:42.020
and see where you
need more data.

00:14:42.020 --> 00:14:45.490
And to actually
use that model, you

00:14:45.490 --> 00:14:47.150
can test it out within the UI.

00:14:47.150 --> 00:14:51.520
So I'll just show you uploading
a mirror selfie of myself.

00:14:51.520 --> 00:14:52.180
That's me.

00:14:55.612 --> 00:14:56.320
And there you go.

00:14:56.320 --> 00:14:57.880
You can see it's never
seen this photo before.

00:14:57.880 --> 00:14:59.672
But it can kind of
guess where my jacket is

00:14:59.672 --> 00:15:02.020
and where my jeans are.

00:15:02.020 --> 00:15:05.050
Now, in addition to just
trying this out in the tool,

00:15:05.050 --> 00:15:06.580
you can call this
model the same way

00:15:06.580 --> 00:15:10.330
we did the other APIs,
either through a curl request

00:15:10.330 --> 00:15:12.845
or in a number of
client libraries.

00:15:12.845 --> 00:15:14.470
So Google handles
all the complications

00:15:14.470 --> 00:15:17.358
of actually hosting
your model in the cloud.

00:15:17.358 --> 00:15:18.775
So let's hop back
onto the slides.

00:15:23.640 --> 00:15:25.620
So what is actually
going on under the hood

00:15:25.620 --> 00:15:27.810
when AutoML builds
that model for you?

00:15:27.810 --> 00:15:30.780
Now this talk is machine
learning without data science

00:15:30.780 --> 00:15:31.380
expertise.

00:15:31.380 --> 00:15:33.100
So this is unnecessary.

00:15:33.100 --> 00:15:34.530
You can cover your ears.

00:15:34.530 --> 00:15:36.450
But in case you're just
curious to know what's

00:15:36.450 --> 00:15:39.870
going on under the
hood, well, our photos

00:15:39.870 --> 00:15:41.580
are being classified
using something

00:15:41.580 --> 00:15:45.070
called a neural network.

00:15:45.070 --> 00:15:48.020
But the first step before we can
feed our data into an algorithm

00:15:48.020 --> 00:15:51.530
is that we have to go from
a photograph to numbers.

00:15:51.530 --> 00:15:54.440
So I suppose we have
a photograph of a cat.

00:15:54.440 --> 00:15:55.910
Well, that photograph
is actually

00:15:55.910 --> 00:15:59.270
made up, if it's digital,
of pixel values--

00:15:59.270 --> 00:16:01.970
red, green, blue, which
correspond to numbers.

00:16:01.970 --> 00:16:04.940
So, using those pixel values,
we can convert our photograph

00:16:04.940 --> 00:16:07.940
into data by making
it into three

00:16:07.940 --> 00:16:12.015
arrays a red, green,
and blue pixel values.

00:16:12.015 --> 00:16:13.640
Then that is the
input to this which is

00:16:13.640 --> 00:16:14.890
a diagram of a neural network.

00:16:14.890 --> 00:16:16.973
And, admittedly, I'm going
to sort of go over this

00:16:16.973 --> 00:16:17.540
very quickly.

00:16:17.540 --> 00:16:19.760
There are other great
talks that talk about it.

00:16:19.760 --> 00:16:22.400
But, basically, what's
happening is those pixel values

00:16:22.400 --> 00:16:25.910
are being added, multiplied,
combined, scaled, and then

00:16:25.910 --> 00:16:30.500
passed to a higher level and
then passed to a higher layer.

00:16:30.500 --> 00:16:35.138
And eventually, a number comes
out that says 80% dog, 20% cat.

00:16:35.138 --> 00:16:36.680
And, at first, the
model doesn't know

00:16:36.680 --> 00:16:38.190
how to combine those numbers.

00:16:38.190 --> 00:16:40.870
But as you show it more
and more training examples,

00:16:40.870 --> 00:16:42.520
it becomes more accurate.

00:16:42.520 --> 00:16:44.270
If you're interested
in this, I definitely

00:16:44.270 --> 00:16:47.460
encourage you to explore more.

00:16:47.460 --> 00:16:49.330
But, normally, training
a model to do this

00:16:49.330 --> 00:16:51.330
if you were to do it as
Torry said from scratch,

00:16:51.330 --> 00:16:54.450
takes a lot of data, probably
more data than we had,

00:16:54.450 --> 00:16:57.480
which was something
like under 200 examples.

00:16:57.480 --> 00:16:59.220
But luckily, when
you use AutoML,

00:16:59.220 --> 00:17:01.080
you're not using just your data.

00:17:01.080 --> 00:17:02.670
You're actually
using Google's models

00:17:02.670 --> 00:17:04.660
that have been trained
on lots more data.

00:17:04.660 --> 00:17:07.210
So you can have a very
high quality model

00:17:07.210 --> 00:17:11.210
even if you don't have that
much data to work with.

00:17:11.210 --> 00:17:13.712
And these neural network
technologies power lots

00:17:13.712 --> 00:17:15.170
of Google machine
learning products

00:17:15.170 --> 00:17:18.560
like Speech-to-Text, that's
transcription, translation,

00:17:18.560 --> 00:17:20.161
and object recognition.

00:17:22.750 --> 00:17:24.680
AutoML also has lots
of different data types

00:17:24.680 --> 00:17:26.849
you can pass
besides just images.

00:17:26.849 --> 00:17:29.210
So let's talk about that.

00:17:29.210 --> 00:17:31.880
TORRY YANG: So, yeah, one of our
new products is AutoML Tables.

00:17:31.880 --> 00:17:34.070
It was just freshly
announced last month

00:17:34.070 --> 00:17:35.510
at Google Cloud Next.

00:17:35.510 --> 00:17:37.550
And what does AutoML Tables do?

00:17:37.550 --> 00:17:41.180
Well, it deals with
structured tabular data.

00:17:41.180 --> 00:17:43.370
So let's just say
there's this table here

00:17:43.370 --> 00:17:49.700
and you have strings,
timestamps, numbers, classes.

00:17:49.700 --> 00:17:53.580
And what you really want is just
predicting the target column.

00:17:53.580 --> 00:17:56.570
So this is actually a
data set of a marketplace.

00:17:56.570 --> 00:18:01.460
And people are selling
things in the marketplace.

00:18:01.460 --> 00:18:06.472
We want to predict how much
those items are sold for.

00:18:06.472 --> 00:18:08.180
Well, it turns out
there's a data science

00:18:08.180 --> 00:18:12.710
competition for just this very
specific task I described.

00:18:12.710 --> 00:18:15.770
And before I tell you how
AutoML Tables performed

00:18:15.770 --> 00:18:18.560
in that competition,
we actually graphed

00:18:18.560 --> 00:18:22.640
the results of every contestant
on this visualization here.

00:18:22.640 --> 00:18:24.690
On the x-axis, we
have the ranking.

00:18:24.690 --> 00:18:27.470
So the more towards the right,
the better the team did.

00:18:27.470 --> 00:18:30.380
And then on the y-axis,
we have the error.

00:18:30.380 --> 00:18:36.140
So the closer to the bottom of
the chart, the lower the error.

00:18:36.140 --> 00:18:39.140
So, if you think about
it, the better contestants

00:18:39.140 --> 00:18:40.910
have lower errors,
which makes sense,

00:18:40.910 --> 00:18:44.240
and the worst contestants
have higher errors.

00:18:44.240 --> 00:18:47.330
And here's how AutoML did.

00:18:47.330 --> 00:18:50.000
In just an hour,
AutoML Tables is

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:53.900
able to surpass the
median competitor.

00:18:53.900 --> 00:18:57.590
And given 11 more hours,
given 23 more hours,

00:18:57.590 --> 00:18:59.840
AutoML can do better.

00:18:59.840 --> 00:19:02.480
And what's interesting and
something I want to point out

00:19:02.480 --> 00:19:06.770
is that all three of these
one hour, 12 hour 24 hours

00:19:06.770 --> 00:19:10.430
is on the plateau, which I
think is where like the data

00:19:10.430 --> 00:19:13.970
science enthusiast and data
science professionals hang out.

00:19:13.970 --> 00:19:16.410
So that's really
cool for AutoML Table

00:19:16.410 --> 00:19:18.450
to be able to
achieve such results.

00:19:20.903 --> 00:19:23.320
DALE MARKOWTIZ: So Torry just
told you about tabular data.

00:19:23.320 --> 00:19:27.198
But what about text, which is an
extremely common type of data.

00:19:27.198 --> 00:19:28.740
For that, we can
build a custom model

00:19:28.740 --> 00:19:30.363
using AutoML Natural Language.

00:19:30.363 --> 00:19:32.280
And there are actually
lots of different types

00:19:32.280 --> 00:19:34.930
of natural language
models that we can build.

00:19:34.930 --> 00:19:37.210
So we could build a
classification model.

00:19:37.210 --> 00:19:40.800
So maybe we have US
congressional bills

00:19:40.800 --> 00:19:44.970
and maybe we want to categorize
them by what they're about.

00:19:44.970 --> 00:19:46.560
We can build a custom
model to do that

00:19:46.560 --> 00:19:48.420
with AutoML natural language.

00:19:48.420 --> 00:19:50.130
And then, at Next last
month, we actually

00:19:50.130 --> 00:19:52.650
released two new types of
models that we can build.

00:19:52.650 --> 00:19:54.840
The first one is
custom sentiment.

00:19:54.840 --> 00:19:57.510
So maybe you remember that Tory
showed you the Natural Language

00:19:57.510 --> 00:19:59.850
API could take a sentence
and tell you whether it

00:19:59.850 --> 00:20:01.645
was positive or negative.

00:20:01.645 --> 00:20:03.270
But if you train your
own custom model,

00:20:03.270 --> 00:20:06.570
then you can learn a very
terminology specific things

00:20:06.570 --> 00:20:09.270
like that waiting
on the tarmac is bad

00:20:09.270 --> 00:20:10.890
but having lots of
leg room is great.

00:20:13.790 --> 00:20:16.430
You can also build a custom
entity extraction model.

00:20:16.430 --> 00:20:19.840
So, again, out of the box,
the API can take a string

00:20:19.840 --> 00:20:21.520
and tell you what entities are.

00:20:21.520 --> 00:20:23.890
For example, it knows
that I, Dale, am a person

00:20:23.890 --> 00:20:25.930
and a cappuccino
is a consumer good.

00:20:25.930 --> 00:20:28.120
And it doesn't really
know what a croissant is.

00:20:28.120 --> 00:20:31.270
But if I wanted to make a model
that really understood foods,

00:20:31.270 --> 00:20:34.210
I could do that, and maybe say
that cappuccino is a beverage

00:20:34.210 --> 00:20:38.227
and identify that
croissant as a food.

00:20:38.227 --> 00:20:40.560
And, to do that, you create
a data set much like the one

00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:42.540
I showed you for
object detection.

00:20:42.540 --> 00:20:44.660
But to do this, you actually
take strings of text

00:20:44.660 --> 00:20:46.910
and you label them in line
and you say this is a food,

00:20:46.910 --> 00:20:48.420
this is a beverage, and so on.

00:20:51.583 --> 00:20:52.750
TORRY YANG: So let's review.

00:20:52.750 --> 00:20:57.160
Today, we showed you that,
if you don't have data,

00:20:57.160 --> 00:21:00.820
you can use one of
Google Cloud AI's APIs.

00:21:00.820 --> 00:21:05.140
But if you have data and you
have your own custom use case,

00:21:05.140 --> 00:21:08.030
you can use Cloud AutoML.

00:21:08.030 --> 00:21:10.120
And we showed you how
to build custom models

00:21:10.120 --> 00:21:12.150
with different data types.

00:21:12.150 --> 00:21:15.060
Here are some links
for you to visit.

00:21:15.060 --> 00:21:16.810
DALE MARKOWTIZ: We
hope what you take away

00:21:16.810 --> 00:21:19.060
from this is that you can
play with machine learning

00:21:19.060 --> 00:21:20.740
while focusing on
building your app

00:21:20.740 --> 00:21:22.990
and not on building your
model because you can get

00:21:22.990 --> 00:21:24.657
started with these
tools really quickly,

00:21:24.657 --> 00:21:25.930
probably in under an hour.

00:21:25.930 --> 00:21:28.450
And you can build
awesome things with them.

00:21:28.450 --> 00:21:31.110
So thanks for coming to listen.

00:21:31.110 --> 00:21:33.580
We'll hang out here to answer
questions right now I guess.

